"The surah was revealed to warn a people to whom no warner had come before. This points to the inter-prophetic period (fatrah). Allah's creating the heavens and the earth in six days and then establishing Himself over the Throne (istiwa') does not signify resting, but rather exercising judgment and dominion.\n\nAllah regulates affairs from the heavens, sending them down to the earth, and then those affairs ascend back to Him. This descent and ascent takes place in a day equal to a thousand years of what you count. This expression of a thousand years figuratively conveys a long span of time; it does not preclude it being less or more.\n\nAllah has made everything He created good. As for man, He created him from an extracted quintessence, from a despised fluid. This points to the beginning of his creation.\n\nOn the Day of Resurrection, the sinners, with their heads bowed down, say: 'Our Lord! We have seen and heard, so return us that we may do righteous deeds; we now believe with certainty.' Allah, however, decrees: 'Had I willed, I would have given guidance to everyone, but the word that I will surely fill Hell with jinn and humans together has come true.' It is said to them: 'Taste the punishment for having forgotten this day of yours; indeed, We too have forgotten you.'\n\nThose who believe in Allah's verses fall down in prostration when they are reminded, glorify and praise their Lord, and do not act arrogantly. Their sides shun their beds, they pray to their Lord in fear and hope, and spend benevolently from the provision given to them. No soul knows what reward is prepared for them; these are things that will be a delight to the eyes.\n\nWhile showing Mu'adh ibn Jabal the gates of goodness, our Prophet (peace be upon him) emphasized that fasting is a shield, charity extinguishes sins, and highlighted the importance of the night prayer, then recited the verses, 'Their sides shun their beds...'\n\nThe believer and the transgressor (fasiq) are not equal. For those who believe and do righteous deeds, there are the Gardens of Refuge (Jannat al-Ma'wa). As for the transgressors, their abode is the Fire. Every time they wish to come out of it, they are turned back into it and told: 'Taste the punishment of the Fire which you used to deny.' Before the greatest punishment, they are also made to taste a nearer punishment in this world so that they might perhaps return.\n\nJust as Moses (Musa) was given the Scripture, you too have been given the Scripture; have no doubt about it. And it was made a guide for the Children of Israel. From among those who were patient, We raised leaders. Your Lord will judge between them on the Day of Resurrection regarding the matters wherein they differed. While the destruction of previous generations is a cautionary lesson, do they still not see that We send down water upon barren land and bring forth crops? And they ask, 'When is that conquest?' Say: 'On that day, faith will not benefit those who disbelieve.' So turn away from them and watch, for they too are watching."